=== Early Life ===
'''Tony Stark''' was the son of technocrat [[Howard Stark (Earth-6160)|Howard Stark]], also known as Iron Man; one of the two heads of the mega-corporation [[Stane/Stark Industries (Earth-6160)|Stane/Stark Industries]], which controlled the geopolitical region known as the [[North America|North American Union]], one of the seven sovereign bodies whose combined power governed the entire world. However, due to his reserved nature, Howard defaulted to his business partner and lifelong friend [[Obadiah Stane (Earth-6160)|Obadiah Stane]] when it came to the company's public relations.{{r|Ultimate Invasion Vol 1 2}}

Inheriting his father's brilliance, Tony was only five years old when he proved mathematically that the world is unfair, causing himself a three-day emotional breakdown. Howard did little to pacify Tony's concerns, only building him a rudimentary psychotherapy AI called [[Just A Rather Very Intelligent System (Earth-6160)|J.A.R.V.I.S.]] before throwing himself back into his work.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 1}} Ever since then, Tony began thinking how to fix the world. One of the first things he came up with was the name for an initiative to do such thing, and he settled on "the Ultimates."{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}}

[[File:Anthony Stark (Earth-6160) from Ultimate Invasion Vol 1 3 001.jpg|thumb|left]]
=== Becoming Iron Lad ===
When Tony was in his late teens, Howard visited [[Latveria]] to attend a convergence of incorporated nation-states for the first time. After Obadiah's death during an assassination attempt on the world's imperator, the [[Reed Richards (Earth-1610)|Maker]], Howard was made privy to the knowledge that the relationships held between the seven bodies were a ruse, as their leaders had actually formed a secret cabal under the leadership of the Maker. They orchestrated global conflicts and alliances to unite the people of each region, guided by the belief that achieving genuine lasting peace was impossible. When returning to the Union for Stane's funeral, Howard shared this discovery with Tony. The Starks were appalled, and Howard was ashamed of his passive compliance. Tony encouraged him to fight against the [[Maker's Council (Earth-6160)|Maker's Council]].

Shortly afterward, Howard was enlisted by the Maker to return to Latveria to repair the [[Immortus Engine]], a time machine secretly used by the imperator to reshape history according to his vision. An invader from another universe, the Maker had used his foreknowledge of the world's status quo to alter history, using a database on super heroes stolen from the alternate reality of [[Earth-616]] to either neutralize or eliminate any possible resistance to his grand design.{{r|Ultimate Invasion Vol 1 3}} Howard made an ally out of a prisoner of the Maker named [[Reed Richards (Earth-6160)|Reed Richards]], also known as Doom. Howard and Doom decided to betray the imperator, facilitating an assault from the Maker's time-travelling enemy [[Anthony Stark (Kang) (Earth-6160)|Kang]]. Howard defeated them both using a duplicate Immortus Engine, causing the Maker's base of operations, the [[Dome (Location)|City]], to seal itself from the world for two years with the Maker, Kang and Howard still inside. Doom escaped beforehand with a copy of the Maker's database and the original damaged Immortus Engine for Tony to repair it, as well as a letter from Howard. With Howard presumed dead, Tony built a battlesuit of his own and adopted the alias of '''Iron Lad''' to follow his father's footsteps. Together with Doom, Iron Lad set out to rectify the Maker's impact in the world.{{r|Ultimate Invasion Vol 1 4}}

=== Fixing the World ===
The duo began their mission in the [[Arctic Circle]], using the Immortus Engine to travel to [[World War II]]. They retrieved the frozen super-soldier [[Steven Rogers (Earth-6160)|Steve Rogers]], aka [[Captain America]], beating the Maker to it, since the imperator had attempted to locate him during his time-travelling quest to stomp superheroism. Iron Lad and Doom next infiltrated the mythological realm of [[Asgard (Realm)|Asgard]] to enlist the captive Norse god of thunder, [[Thor Odinson (Earth-6160)|Thor]], to their cause. His warden [[Sif (Earth-6160)|Lady Sif]] joined the trio in their return to Earth as a stowaway. During a subsequent raid on a repository of confiscated superhuman catalysts in Latveria, the heroes were intercepted by a member of the Maker's Council, [[Henri Dugarry (Earth-6160)|Captain Britain]], who critically injured Thor. Iron Lad managed to fend off Captain Britain, costing the villain an eye before escaping back to [[Stark Tower]]. Now alert of the young Stark's activities, the Maker's Council hijacked a Stane/Stark orbital station and fired it at Stark Tower. Tony used the Immortus Engine to teleport away with his allies and the still-frozen Captain America, escaping six months into the future. The Council pinned the attack on Stark, branding him as a terrorist.{{r|Ultimate Universe Vol 1 1}}

[[File:Anthony Stark (Earth-6160) from Ultimate Invasion Vol 1 4 002.jpg|thumb|right|220px|Tony becomes Iron Lad]]
Arriving on June 2024, Tony and his allies made a new base out of an off-the-books corporate spy satellite that became dubbed the [[Triskelion (Satellite)|Triskelion]]. Iron Lad posited the creation of a super hero resistance network, the Ultimates, sending the stolen catalysts six months into the past to those whose destiny the Maker had tampered with, so as to have their army retroactively exist in their new present. However, the plan mostly failed; most of these "[[Origin Box]]es" were rejected, intercepted, or their recipients accidentally killed by their new-found powers.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 1}} The only exceptions were reporter [[Peter Parker (Earth-6160)|Peter Parker]]{{r|Ultimate Spider-Man Vol 3 1}} and prisoner [[Lucas Cage (Earth-6160)|Luke Cage]].{{r|Ultimate Spider-Man Vol 3 9}} Swearing off the Immortus Engine, Tony moved to the "Ultimates 2.0," taking a more active approach in recruitment, as suggested by Captain America, who Tony eventually had managed to thaw out. Their first recruits were a couple of exterminators, [[Henry Pym (Earth-6160)|Hank Pym]] and [[Janet Van Dyne (Earth-6160)|Janet Van Dyne]], who became Giant-Man and the Wasp.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 1}}

The Ultimates next raided the [[White House]] to liberate [[America Chavez (Earth-6160)|America Chavez]], an amnesiac time-travelling refugee from the future whose ability to generate cosmic rays was being exploited by the uber-capitalist [[Midas (Earth-6160)|Midas]] as an energy source.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 2}} The following missions were smaller in scale and handled by smaller strike teams. Alongside Doom and Captain America, Tony raided a [[Damage Control (Earth-6160)|Damage Control]] warehouse to reactivate the android [[Jim Hammond (Earth-6160)|Human Torch]].{{r|Free Comic Book Day 2024: Ultimate Universe/Spider-Man Vol 1 1}} Later on, Tony enlisted Thor and Sif to investigate the irradiated [[Gamma Island]] in the [[Pacific Ocean]], where they recruited the native [[Lejori Zakaria (Earth-6160)|Lejori Zakaria]], aka [[She-Hulk]], in exchange for decontaminating her homeland.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 3}} Shortly after, Tony checked in on Peter Parker, now known as [[Spider-Man]]. Peter had teamed up with [[Harold Osborn (Earth-6160)|Harry Osborn]], also known as the Green Goblin, to take down the [[Wilson Fisk (Earth-6160)|Kingpin]], who was the Council's representative in [[New York City]].{{r|Ultimate Spider-Man Vol 3 8}} The final member of the Ultimates 2.0 came in the unexpected form of [[Charli Ramsey (Earth-6160)|Charli Ramsey]], a member of the Oglala Lakota Nation who appropriated the equipment from the Origin Box discarded by [[Clinton Barton (Earth-6160)|Clint Barton]], becoming Hawkeye in Barton's stead. Fearing the gear had fallen in the wrong hands, Tony asked Captain America to retrieve it, but Ramsey proved a hero in their own right.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 6}}

In November 2024, Iron Lad assembled the entirety of the Ultimates Network, minus Doom, for the first time in an attempt to rescue super-powered individuals from a Damage Control black site. However, the intel was bait, and the Ultimates were lured through a portal to [[K'un-Lun]], where they were confronted by another member of the Maker's Council, the [[Bruce Banner (Earth-6160)|Hulk]], and his Gamma-powered [[Immortal Weapons (Earth-6160)|Immortal Weapons]].{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 6}} The Ultimates were overpowered and would have been killed if not for Doom using the Immortus Engine to reverse the outcome. Although Richards managed to whisk the team away from the Hulk's clutches mostly unscathed, his attempt to avoid a full timeline divergence meant he couldn't stop Tony from recieving fatal injuries.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 12}} Tony was placed in suspended animation, remaining out of commission for three months.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 12}} During this time, Thor and Sif left the Ultimates to wage their own revolution across the [[Nine Realms]].{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 10}}

[[File:Anthony Stark (Earth-6160) from Ultimates Vol 3 7 001.jpg|thumb|left|220px|Tony in critical state]]

=== Rescue Mission ===
Tony underwent several surgeries to repair the extensive damage from his bout with the Hulk, but what saved his life was fusing the Immortus Engine to his nervous system,{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 10}} freezing himself forever in the decisecond before death, also allowing him to experience time non-linearly. Tony found out of Doom's reckless use of the Immortal Engine in his rescue from K'un-Lun and forced him to come clean to the rest of the team. To Doom's surprise, Tony approved of his intentions, taking the chance to take a step back and reassess the performance of the Ultimates. Its members were reinvigorated as they agreed for more active outreach to expand the network, and a community-oriented approach.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 12}} The Ultimates 3.0 reinvented the network into a grassroots counter-cultural movement, seeking to maximize human flourishing and cooperation, covertly educating and organizing citizens around the world, and fighting the propaganda of the Maker's Council.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}}

[[File:Ultimates (Earth-6160) from Ultimate Spider-Man Incursion Vol 1 5.jpg|thumb|220px|Tony and the other Ultimates face off against Da Costa]]
When [[Miles Morales (Earth-1610)|Miles Morales]] and his little sister, [[Billie Morales (Earth-616)|Billie]], arrived separately from Earth-616, the Ultimates—including Tony—detected their dimensional signature aboard the Triskelion. Captain America knew that the Council would try to capture the young alternate Spider-Man, so he sent the Wasp and Giant-Man to New York City to retrieve him.{{r|Ultimate Spider-Man: Incursion Vol 1 1}} The search for Billie took Miles around the world and ended in Latveria near the City. Council member [[Emmanuel Da Costa (Earth-6160)|Emmanuel Da Costa]] had captured her, believing that she was the key to prematurely freeing the Maker. The Ultimates intercepted Da Costa before he could free the Maker, and they fought him. Amidst the battle, Tony, Steve, and Miles discussed how to handle newly discovered, immensely powerful superheroic catalysts. They ultimately agreed to store them in Origin Boxes and give them to Miles on his way back to his home reality that he could place them under the care of his version of the Ultimates, known as the [[Avengers (Earth-616)|Avengers]].{{r|Ultimate Spider-Man: Incursion Vol 1 5}}

With Luke Cage having inspired prison uprisings across North America and turning them into rehabilitative sovereign states, Tony used the Immortus Engine to create time bubbles similar to the City, where time passed faster than outside. In these collectives, the Ultimates trained the next generation of super heroes through the [[Avengers (Earth-6160)|Ultimate Avengers]] Program. As the Ultimates 3.0 took shape, Tony sent the Human Torch back in time so that he could help the revolutionary efforts under the radar through decades of secret work. Iron Lad also made use of the Immortus Engine to create time clones of himself, allowing him to be in numerous places at the same time.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}} During this time, Tony led a team into the [[Tibet]], where they recruited the prophetic [[Shen Qi (Earth-6160)|Shen Qi]], the [[Iron Fist]].{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 15}} In November 2025, with only one month left to the opening of the City, Tony hijacked communications worldwide to broadcast a rousing message across the globe. Revealing himself to the public for the first time, Iron Lad exposed the true history of the world, the existence of the Maker's Council and expounded the mission and efforts of the Ultimates to fight back. Tony's speech was a success in encouraging people to fight against the status quo and its many injustices, prompting civilians worldwide to rise up.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 18}}

| Personality             = 
{{Quote|I '''propose '''we put right what the [[Reed Richards (Earth-1610)|Maker]] put wrong. To help this [[Earth-6160|world]] become the world it is '''supposed''' to be. [[Earth-616|A world of '''heroes''']].|[[Anthony Stark (Earth-6160)|Iron Lad]]|Ultimates Vol 3 1}}
Despite his awareness of the unfairness of the world, Tony is a wide-eyed and precocious idealist, setting on the goal to fix it since he was five years old.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}} When he became privy to the true workings of the world and the fact that the status quo had been insidiously orchestrated by the [[Reed Richards (Earth-1610)|Maker]], Tony was quick to express a righteous desire to undo the imperator's vision.{{r|Ultimate Invasion Vol 1 3}} This uprightness extends to Stark's own self-accountability, expressing extreme dismay when his plan to deliver [[Origin Box]]es to potential heroes resulted in the unintended death of a fair share of the recipients,{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 1}}{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 5}} or feeling responsibility for [[Gamma Island]] coming under the scrutiny of the [[Maker's Council (Earth-6160)|Maker's Council]] after he made allies out of their inhabitants.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}}

Tony is deeply science-oriented, putting his faith on the calculations behind the Ultimates 1.0 to yield its intended result, shooting down [[Steven Rogers (Earth-6160)|Captain America]]'s concerns about its chance of success in the process,{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 1}} and becoming fascinated with the mutated fauna of Gamma Island when he first visited, wishing to alter the ecosystem as little as possible.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 3}} Despite his ambitious ideals, Stark is adverse to risk,{{r|Ultimate Universe Vol 1 1}} with the [[Jim Hammond (Earth-6160)|Human Torch]] once pointing out that Iron Lad does "too much thinking, not enough doing." However, following his death and resurrection, Tony took a more proactive approach to the Ultimates initiative, expressing open-mindedness to other people's ideas and favoring a community-oriented method to connect with people.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}}

Stark is also devoted to his work to an acute degree; resorting to his armor's life support systems to avoid eating or sleeping for six days when engineering the Origin Boxes,{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 1}} or undergoing surgery while conscious and without anesthesia to instead monitor the Ultimates.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 9}} When he embedded the Immortus Engine to his body, Tony used it to stop eating and sleeping altogether, also employing the machine to break himself down so as to be able to exist simultaneously in different places at the same time to maximize his productivity.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}} 

| Powers                  =
{{Quote|We have a '''time machine'''. It's time to really '''use''' it.|[[Anthony Stark (Earth-6160)|Iron Lad]]|Ultimates Vol 3 13}}
[[File:Anthony Stark (Earth-6160) from Ultimates Vol 3 13 001.jpg|thumb|right|220px|Tony using the Immortus Engine to break himself down]]  
* '''{{Power|Time Manipulation}}:''' Ever since he fused his nervous system with the [[Immortus Engine]], Tony can access its time-travelling capabilities on his own,{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 10}} being able to {{Power|Remote Time Traveling|send}} people and {{Power|Time Traveling|himself}} through time. Tony can also experience time non-linearly and observe the {{Power|Precognition|future}} and the {{Power|Retrocognition|past}}. He can additionally generate spheres where time passes at a different ratio,{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}} known as Darwin Bubbles,{{r|Ultimate Endgame Vol 1 2}} like 6-to-1 relative to the outside world and {{Power|Temporal Duplication|create}} time-duplicates of himself that share his consciousness.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}} These time duplicates exists independently from Tony to an extent. Even though he experiences what they go through, he doesn't share their pain and they can die without affecting his own well-being.{{r|Ultimates Vol 3 13}}

